{
    "text": "lay",
    "etymologies": [
        {
            "etymologyHTML": "<p>From Middle English <i class="Latn mention" lang="enm">leyen</i>, <i class="Latn mention" lang="enm">leggen</i>, from Old English <i class="Latn mention" lang="ang">leċġan</i> (“to lay”), from Proto-West Germanic <i class="Latn mention" lang="gmw-pro">*laggjan</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i class="Latn mention" lang="gem-pro">*lagjaną</i> (“to lay”), causative form of Proto-Germanic <i class="Latn mention" lang="gem-pro">*ligjaną</i> (“to lie, recline”), from Proto-Indo-European <i class="Latn mention" lang="ine-pro">*legʰ-</i> (“to lie, recline”).\n</p><p>Cognate with West Frisian <i class="Latn mention" lang="fy">lizze</i> (“to lay, to lie”), Dutch <i class="Latn mention" lang="nl">leggen</i> (“to lay”), German <i class="Latn mention" lang="de">legen</i> (“to lay”), Norwegian Bokmål <i class="Latn mention" lang="nb">legge</i> (“to lay”), Norwegian Nynorsk <i class="Latn mention" lang="nn">leggja</i> (“to lay”), Swedish <i class="Latn mention" lang="sv">lägga</i> (“to lay”), Icelandic <i class="Latn mention" lang="is">leggja</i> (“to lay”), Albanian <i class="Latn mention" lang="sq">lag</i> (“troop, band, war encampment”).\n</p>",
            "definitions": []
        },
        {
            "etymologyHTML": "<p>From Middle English <i class="Latn mention" lang="enm">laie</i>, <i class="Latn mention" lang="enm">lawe</i>, from Old English <i class="Latn mention" lang="ang">lagu</i> (“sea, flood, water, ocean”), from Proto-West Germanic <i class="Latn mention" lang="gmw-pro">*lagu</i> (“water, sea”), from Proto-Germanic <i class="Latn mention" lang="gem-pro">*laguz</i> (“water, sea”), from Proto-Indo-European <i class="Latn mention" lang="ine-pro">*lókus</i> (“water, body of water, lake”). Cognate with Icelandic <i class="Latn mention" lang="is">lögur</i> (“liquid, fluid, lake”), Latin <i class="Latn mention" lang="la">lacus</i> (“lake, hollow, hole”).\n</p>",
            "definitions": []
        },
        {
            "etymologyHTML": "<p>From Middle English <i class="Latn mention" lang="enm">lay</i>, from Old French <i class="Latn mention" lang="fro">lai</i>, from Latin <i class="Latn mention" lang="la">laicus</i>, from Ancient Greek <i class="Polyt mention" lang="grc">λαϊκός</i> (laïkós). Doublet of <i class="Latn mention" lang="en">laic</i>.\n</p>",
            "definitions": []
        },
        {
            "etymologyHTML": "<p>See <i class="Latn mention" lang="en">lie</i>. This word was influenced by the present tense verb <i class="Latn mention" lang="en">lay</i>.\n</p>",
            "definitions": []
        },
        {
            "etymologyHTML": "<p>From Middle English <i class="Latn mention" lang="enm">lay</i>, from Old French <i class="Latn mention" lang="fro">lai</i> (“song, lyric, poem”), from Frankish <i class="Latn mention" lang="gmw-pro">*laih</i> (“play, melody, song”), from Proto-Germanic <i class="Latn mention" lang="gem-pro">*laikaz</i>, <i class="Latn mention" lang="gem-pro">*laikiz</i> (“jump, play, dance, hymn”), from Proto-Indo-European <i class="Latn mention" lang="ine-pro">*leyg-</i> (“to jump, spring, play”). Akin to Old High German <i class="Latn mention" lang="goh">leih</i> (“a play, skit, melody, song”), Middle High German <i class="Latn mention" lang="gmh">leich</i> (“piece of music, epic song played on a harp”), Old English <i class="Latn mention" lang="ang">lācan</i> (“to move quickly, fence, sing”). See <i class="Latn mention" lang="en">lake</i> (“to play”).\n</p>",
            "definitions": []
        },
        {
            "etymologyHTML": "<p>From Middle English <i class="Latn mention" lang="enm">lay</i>, <i class="Latn mention" lang="enm">laye</i>, <i class="Latn mention" lang="enm">laiȝe</i>, <i class="Latn mention" lang="enm">leyȝe</i>, from Old English <i class="Latn mention" lang="ang">lǣh</i>, <i class="Latn mention" lang="enm">lēh</i>, northern (Anglian) variants of Old English <i class="Latn mention" lang="ang">lēah</i> (“lea”). More at <i class="Latn mention" lang="en">lea</i>.\n</p>",
            "definitions": []
        },
        {
            "etymologyHTML": "<p>From Middle English <i class="Latn mention" lang="enm">laige</i>, <i class="Latn mention" lang="enm">læȝe</i>, variants of Middle English <i class="Latn mention" lang="enm">lawe</i> (“law”). More at <i class="Latn mention" lang="en">law</i>.\n</p>",
            "definitions": []
        },
        {
            "etymologyHTML": "<p>Calque of Yiddish <i class="Hebr mention" lang="yi">לייגן</i> (leygn, “to put, lay”).\n</p>",
            "definitions": []
        }
    ],
    "pronunciation": []
